<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (Inserting a late reading into the middle of a Python list costs O\(n\) because later elements must shift. A list is still reasonable for a year of 365 readings and occasional corrections. For frequent insertions at both ends, collections.deque may help, but a deque is not ideal for arbitrary date-based insertion. A dictionary keyed by date gives average O\(1\) lookup by date, but chronological reports require sorting the dates, usually O\(n log n\). )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 142 0 R
/E (A deque\(maxlen=7\) is useful for maintaining the current seven-reading window in a streaming system. With a running sum, each new reading can update the average in O\(1\); processing n readings is O\(n\). A full list stores O\(n\) readings; a seven-element deque window uses O\(7\) = O\(1\) extra window space. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
